LESSONS = [
    {
        'id': 'security-governance-ethics-legal',
        'title': 'Security Governance, Ethics, Compliance and Privacy',
        'summary': 'How security is directed from the top, the ethics every CISSP is held to, and the legal and privacy duties that shape every decision.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f1', 'f3', 'f4', 'f5', 'f6', 'f9', 'f10', 'f15'],
        'quizIds': ['q1', 'q2', 'q3', 'q4', 'q5', 'q18'],
        'reading': """Security governance answers a simple question: who decides what the organization protects, how much risk it will live with, and who is held accountable? The answer is senior management and the board. Governance is the direction and oversight they provide; management then turns that direction into plans, budgets and daily work. This is why the CISSP exam favors a top-down approach. A security program that starts with a technician's good idea but has no executive sponsor has no authority, no budget and no way to enforce anything. Whenever a scenario shows confusion, conflicting rules or cut funding, the best first move is nearly always to get senior-level direction and align security with the business goals.

Direction is written down in layers. A policy is a short, high-level, mandatory statement of management intent, such as "all laptops must be encrypted". Standards say precisely what is required, a baseline is the minimum configuration, procedures are the step-by-step instructions, and guidelines are optional advice. Reading from the top, each layer gets more specific; only guidelines are voluntary.

Underneath everything sit the core goals of security: confidentiality, integrity and availability, often called the CIA triad, plus authenticity and nonrepudiation. Access is controlled with AAA: authentication proves who you are, authorization decides what you may do, and accounting records what you did. Organizations choose a framework to organize their controls, such as NIST CSF (which in version 2.0 has six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover), NIST SP 800-53, ISO/IEC 27001 (the standard an organization can be certified against), COBIT for IT governance, or SABSA for architecture.

Two ideas define professional responsibility. Due diligence is the research: understanding the risks and the options before acting. Due care is the action: doing what a reasonable person would do with that knowledge. An organization that studied a weakness and then did nothing has shown diligence but failed in due care, which is negligence. Ethics extends this duty to you personally. The ISC2 Code of Ethics has four canons, listed in order of precedence: protect society, the common good, necessary public trust and confidence, and the infrastructure; act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally; provide diligent and competent service to principals; and advance and protect the profession. When duties collide, the earlier canon wins.

Finally, security professionals work inside a legal environment. Intellectual property is protected through patents, copyrights, trademarks and trade secrets. Privacy laws attach to the data and the people it describes, not to where your servers sit: the EU's GDPR covers EU residents' data even when processed by a company elsewhere, and it requires breach notification to the regulator within 72 hours. In the United States, sector laws apply: HIPAA for health information, GLBA for financial institutions, SOX for public-company reporting, FERPA for student records and COPPA for children's data. PCI DSS, by contrast, is a contractual industry standard rather than a law.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to security governance? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Think of an organization as a ship. The board and executives are the owners who decide the destination and how much risk the voyage may take; that is governance. The captain and officers turn those orders into routes, rules and duty rosters; that is management. The crew follow procedures to keep the ship safe each day. If the crew make up their own rules with no instruction from the owners, the ship might still float, but nobody has the authority to enforce a safety drill or pay for lifeboats. Due diligence is the officer who studies the weather charts; due care is the officer who actually changes course when a storm is coming.",
        'keyTerms': ['Governance', 'Policy', 'Standard', 'Due care', 'Due diligence', 'CIA triad', 'AAA', 'ISC2 Code of Ethics', 'GDPR', 'Top-down approach'],
        'commonTraps': "Mixing up due care (acting) and due diligence (researching) is the most common slip. Another is picking a technical fix when the scenario is really asking about management direction, such as policy approval and business alignment. Do not treat PCI DSS as a law, and do not assume GDPR depends on where your company is located. On ethics questions, remember the canon order: public safety outranks duty to the employer, but the right response is measured escalation, not publicity or silence.",
        'scenario': "A mid-sized insurer's regional offices each maintain their own password rules and incident procedures. After a minor breach, the board asks why a known weakness in the claims portal was documented a year earlier but never fixed. The CISO proposes a company-wide security policy approved by the CEO, a governance committee reporting to the board, and standards and procedures beneath the policy, and notes that the portal's EU customers bring GDPR obligations into scope.",
        'onTheJob': "Get executive sponsorship in writing before you build anything else; a policy signed by the CEO ends many arguments before they start. Translate every security request into business language: impact, regulatory exposure, revenue at risk. Keep a map of which laws apply to which data sets, reviewed with legal counsel, because the question is never just \"are we secure?\" but \"are we compliant, and can we prove it?\". Keep your own ethics file too: write down escalations and decisions, because they protect you as much as the organization.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'risk-management-threat-modeling',
        'title': 'Risk Management, Threat Modeling and Supply Chain Risk',
        'summary': 'How to find, measure and treat risk, model threats before systems are built, and manage the risks that arrive through vendors.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f23', 'f25', 'f26', 'f27', 'f28', 'f29', 'f30', 'f31'],
        'quizIds': ['q6', 'q7', 'q8', 'q9', 'q11', 'q12'],
        'reading': """Risk is the chance that something bad happens to something you value, and how bad it would be. Security professionals separate three ingredients. An asset is anything of value, a vulnerability is a weakness, and a threat is anything that could exploit it. Risk exists only when all three line up: a strong vault with no threats is not a risk, and a threat with nothing to exploit is not a risk either. Controls, also called safeguards or countermeasures, reduce risk by closing vulnerabilities or reducing threat impact. Because you can never reach zero, what remains after the controls is called residual risk, and the organization's appetite decides whether that remainder is acceptable.

Risk analysis comes in two styles. Qualitative analysis rates risks as high, medium or low using judgement and workshops; it is quick and easy to understand but subjective. Quantitative analysis puts numbers on it. The key formulas are single loss expectancy (SLE), which is asset value times exposure factor, and annualized rate of occurrence (ARO), the expected number of events per year. Multiplying them gives annualized loss expectancy (ALE). A control is worth buying when the ALE before it, minus the ALE after it, minus its yearly cost is positive. If a 400,000 dollar asset would lose a quarter of its value in a flood expected once every five years, the SLE is 100,000, the ARO is 0.2 and the ALE is 20,000 dollars.

Once risks are understood, management picks a treatment. You can mitigate by adding controls, transfer by using insurance or a contract, avoid by stopping the risky activity, or accept by knowingly living with it. Acceptance is always a business decision recorded by the risk owner; the security team recommends, it does not decide. Insurance transfers the financial cost but not the accountability, and ignoring a risk is not acceptance. For federal and many other organizations, the NIST Risk Management Framework gives a repeatable path: Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor. The authorizing official is the person who accepts the remaining risk and approves the system to operate.

Threat modeling moves this thinking to the design stage, where fixes are cheapest. STRIDE asks, for each part of a design, whether an attacker could spoof an identity, tamper with data, repudiate an action, disclose information, deny service or elevate privilege. DREAD rates the seriousness of what you find. PASTA works through seven stages from business objectives to attack simulation, and attack trees draw the paths an attacker might take to a goal. The point is not to pick one favorite but to recognize each by its purpose.

Today a great deal of risk arrives through other organizations. Supply chain risk management covers counterfeit hardware, tampered software updates and a vendor's weak security. Good practice includes due diligence before choosing a supplier, security and breach-notification terms written into the contract, a right-to-audit clause, a software bill of materials for code you embed, signature checks on updates, and continuous monitoring of critical vendors. A requirement that is not in the contract cannot be enforced, so push security into the agreement before it is signed.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to risk management? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Imagine you own a house near a river. The house is the asset, the cracked foundation is the vulnerability, and the river flooding is the threat. You can mitigate by building a flood wall, transfer the cost by buying flood insurance, avoid the risk by selling the house, or accept it because the river has not flooded in fifty years. Even after the wall you still have some leftover risk, the residual risk. You would not spend more on the wall each year than the expected annual flood damage; that is the same logic as ALE. And if the builder you hired used cheap fake concrete, that is supply chain risk, which is why you check the supplier before the contract is signed.",
        'keyTerms': ['Risk', 'Threat', 'Vulnerability', 'ALE', 'Residual risk', 'Risk appetite', 'Risk treatment', 'Risk Management Framework', 'STRIDE', 'Supply chain risk'],
        'commonTraps': "Insurance is transfer, not elimination. Residual risk is not zero risk, and only management can accept it. Qualitative does not mean worse, only less precise. Do not confuse STRIDE (categories of threats) with DREAD (a scoring scheme). When a safeguard seems expensive, compare its annual cost to the reduction in ALE rather than to the asset's total value. And remember that the authorizing official, not the assessor or administrator, makes the risk-based decision to operate.",
        'scenario': "A company evaluates a 30,000 dollar annual web application firewall subscription. The current ALE for web application attacks is 90,000 dollars, and the vendor claims it would fall to 50,000. The CFO also asks whether buying cyber insurance means the project can be dropped, and procurement wants to buy cheaper network devices from an unvetted reseller to save money.",
        'onTheJob': "Keep a living risk register with an owner for each entry, and rerun the numbers whenever the business changes. Do not oversell quantitative precision: your inputs are estimates, so show ranges. Get risk acceptance signed by the person with the budget and authority, with an expiry date, so it is reviewed rather than forgotten. Insert security language into vendor contracts and master agreements early; negotiating after a vendor is entrenched rarely works.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'continuity-personnel-security',
        'title': 'Business Continuity, Disaster Recovery and Personnel Security',
        'summary': 'Planning to keep the business running through disruption, recovery targets and sites, and controls that manage the people risk.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f33', 'f34', 'f35', 'f36', 'f37', 'f38', 'f32'],
        'quizIds': ['q13', 'q14', 'q15', 'q16', 'q17', 'q34'],
        'reading': """Business continuity planning keeps the critical functions of the organization running through a disruption, and disaster recovery planning is the part that restores IT systems, data and facilities afterwards. Neither starts with technology. They start with the business impact analysis, or BIA, which asks the business owners which processes matter most, what each depends on, and how much it would hurt to lose them over time. Only after the BIA do you choose recovery strategies; skipping it means guessing which systems to save first. Whenever a plan is described, the first priority is always the safety of people.

The BIA produces recovery targets that you must memorize. Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD, also called MAO) is the longest a business function can be unavailable before the organization's survival is threatened. The recovery time objective (RTO) is the time you aim to restore service, the recovery point objective (RPO) is how much data loss, measured in time, is acceptable, and the work recovery time (WRT) is the extra time to verify and reconfigure systems after restoration. RTO plus WRT must fit within the MTD, and a short RPO demands frequent backups or continuous replication. If a call center can be down for 8 hours but needs 6 hours to restore and 4 more to verify, the plan fails its own target.

Targets drive the choice of alternate processing site. A hot site is fully equipped and nearly in sync, recovering in minutes to hours at a high cost. A warm site has hardware and connectivity but needs data restored, recovering in hours to days. A cold site is just space and utilities, taking days to weeks, and mobile sites are relocatable trailers. Reciprocal agreements with another organization are cheap but hard to enforce. Pick the least expensive option that still meets the RTO; a hot site for a 36-hour requirement is waste, while a cold site for a 4-hour one is failure.

A plan that is never tested is a hope. Tests run from a read-through of the document, through a tabletop or structured walkthrough where participants talk through a scenario, a simulation, a parallel test that brings the recovery environment up alongside production, up to a full interruption test that actually shuts production down. Parallel tests are a popular balance because they prove recovery without risking operations. Plans must also be maintained when the business, staff or technology changes, and training ensures the right people know their roles.

People are both the greatest asset and a major source of risk, so personnel security matters. Hiring involves screening and background checks proportionate to the role, along with signed agreements such as NDAs. During employment, separation of duties splits critical tasks so collusion is needed for fraud, least privilege limits access, and job rotation and mandatory vacation expose irregularities that only the current holder of a role can hide. At departure the key is speed: disable accounts, retrieve badges and devices, and remind the employee of continuing obligations. For third parties, contracts and service level agreements must carry security duties, because outsourcing the work never outsources the accountability.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to business continuity? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "A family planning for a house fire does it in a sensible order. First they decide what matters (people, pets, photos, documents): that is the BIA. Then they set targets: everyone out within two minutes, and photos backed up to the cloud weekly. They choose a meeting spot across the street, a bit like picking a hot, warm or cold site, depending on how fast they need to resume normal life. Finally they practice the drill, starting with talking it through at the dinner table and later walking out of the house with the lights off. A plan nobody has practiced is just a piece of paper.",
        'keyTerms': ['Business impact analysis', 'MTD', 'RTO', 'RPO', 'Hot site', 'Warm site', 'Cold site', 'Parallel test', 'Separation of duties', 'Mandatory vacation'],
        'commonTraps': "RPO is about data loss, not time to restore; RTO is the opposite. The BIA comes before the strategy, and business owners supply the impact figures. People's safety outranks equipment in every plan. Do not choose the hottest site by reflex: choose the cheapest that satisfies the RTO. A parallel test does not interrupt production, but a full interruption test does. And mandatory vacation is not a perk; it is a detective control for fraud.",
        'scenario': "A regional bank's online platform must be restored within 12 hours and can lose at most 5 minutes of transactions. The disaster recovery lead proposes a warm site with replication every 30 minutes. During the review, an auditor notes that the same administrator creates payees and approves payments and has not taken a vacation in four years.",
        'onTheJob': "Run the BIA with the people who own the processes, not just IT, and write the numbers down in a form executives will sign. Test at least annually and after major changes, and record what failed; the failures are the value. Keep call trees, vendor contacts and recovery credentials available offline. Fix separation of duties conflicts before an auditor or a fraudster finds them, and make offboarding a checklist owned jointly by HR, IT and security.",
    },
]


LESSONS += [
    {
        'id': 'asset-classification-data-lifecycle',
        'title': 'Asset Classification, Data Lifecycle and Privacy Controls',
        'summary': 'Knowing what you have, deciding how sensitive it is, protecting it through its whole life and destroying it properly at the end.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f39', 'f40', 'f41', 'f42', 'f47', 'f50', 'f51', 'f52'],
        'quizIds': ['q35', 'q36', 'q37', 'q38', 'q39', 'q42'],
        'reading': """Asset security begins with a plain idea: you cannot protect what you do not know you have. An accurate inventory of hardware, software, data and services, each with a named owner, is the foundation. Next comes classification, which assigns each information asset a sensitivity level, for example public, internal, confidential and restricted, or the equivalents used by governments. The level matters only because it drives handling rules: how the data is stored, who can see it, how it travels, how long it is kept and how it is destroyed. Classification is a business decision driven by value and legal duty, so the data owner decides, not the administrator.

Roles are a frequent exam topic. The data owner is the senior person accountable for the data, who sets its classification, retention and who may access it. The data custodian carries out the protections, such as backups, patching and access control operations, according to the owner's requirements. In privacy law the vocabulary shifts: the controller decides why and how personal data is processed, the processor works on the controller's behalf, and the data subject is the person the data describes.

Information has a lifecycle: it is created or collected, stored, used, shared, archived and finally destroyed. Controls must follow it through each stage. Data at rest is protected by encryption of disks, databases and backups. Data in transit is protected with TLS or IPsec. Data in use is the hardest, since it generally has to be decrypted in memory to be processed, which is why secure enclaves and strict access controls matter. Labeling helps people and tools handle each item correctly, and data loss prevention inspects content as it moves to block or flag unauthorized sharing of patterns such as card or national ID numbers.

Privacy-oriented techniques reduce what an attacker or an insider can do with data. Minimization means collecting and keeping only what is needed, and purpose limitation means using it only for the reason it was collected. Tokenization replaces a value, such as a card number, with a random surrogate while the real value stays in a protected vault. Masking hides part of a value for display or testing. Anonymization irreversibly removes the link to a person, so the result is no longer personal data, while pseudonymization uses a reversible code and the data stays regulated. Retention schedules say how long to keep each record, and a legal hold suspends deletion when litigation is reasonably anticipated.

The last stage is where many organizations fail. Deleting a file or quick-formatting a drive leaves recoverable data behind, called data remanence. NIST SP 800-88 describes three levels: clear (overwrite with standard commands), purge (stronger techniques such as cryptographic erase, which destroys the encryption key, or degaussing for magnetic media) and destroy (shred, disintegrate or incinerate). Degaussing does nothing to solid-state drives, so those need cryptographic erase, a verified secure-erase command or physical destruction. Choose the level by sensitivity and by whether the media leaves your control.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to asset security? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Think of a hospital's paper records. Each folder is labeled routine, sensitive or highly restricted, and the label tells staff where it may be stored, who may read it, and whether it can leave the building. The head physician (the owner) decides how sensitive each folder is; the records clerk (the custodian) files and locks them accordingly. Old folders are not just thrown in the bin: highly restricted ones go through a cross-cut shredder, and when a lawsuit is looming, a notice goes out to stop shredding anything related. Digital data deserves the same lifecycle thinking, from the moment the folder is created until the confetti is swept up.",
        'keyTerms': ['Data classification', 'Data owner', 'Data custodian', 'Data lifecycle', 'Data remanence', 'Purge', 'Cryptographic erase', 'Tokenization', 'Pseudonymization', 'Legal hold'],
        'commonTraps': "The owner classifies the data; the custodian does not. Pseudonymized data is still personal data, but anonymized data is not. Degaussing works on magnetic media only, never on SSDs. Quick-formatting and deleting are not sanitization. A legal hold overrides a retention schedule. And masking is for display and testing, tokenization uses a vault, encryption uses a key: do not use the words interchangeably.",
        'scenario': "A hospital is replacing a rack of SSDs that held patient records and plans to sell the old drives to a reseller. A research group has asked for patient data to analyze outcomes, and the legal team has just told IT that a malpractice suit involving closed accounts is likely. The data steward asks which data can be released and how the old drives should be handled.",
        'onTheJob': "Start classification with the datasets that matter most, usually regulated data and crown jewels, rather than trying to label everything. Make labels visible to tools so DLP and cloud controls can act on them. Put sanitization requirements into contracts with disposal vendors and demand certificates of destruction. Review retention schedules with legal counsel each year, because keeping data forever is a liability, not a safety net.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'security-models-design-principles',
        'title': 'Security Models, Secure Design Principles and Physical Security',
        'summary': 'The formal models and design principles behind secure systems, the trust in hardware and cloud architectures, and the physical layer that underlies it all.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f61', 'f62', 'f63', 'f64', 'f67', 'f68', 'f69', 'f71', 'f92'],
        'quizIds': ['q47', 'q48', 'q51', 'q52', 'q53', 'q66'],
        'reading': """Security architecture is the discipline of building protection into a system from the start. The CISSP exam expects you to recognize a handful of design principles and apply them to scenarios. Least privilege gives every subject only the access it needs. Defense in depth layers independent controls so that one failure does not expose everything. Fail securely means that when a control breaks it defaults to a safe state. Secure defaults, separation of duties, economy of mechanism (keep it simple), complete mediation (check every access every time), open design (security should not depend on secrecy of the design) and privacy by design are the others you will see. Zero trust applies the same mindset to networks: no implicit trust because of location, and every request is verified using identity, device posture and context, as described in NIST SP 800-207.

Formal security models describe how a system can enforce a policy. Bell-LaPadula is about confidentiality: a subject cannot read up (simple security property) and cannot write down (star property), which stops secrets flowing to lower levels. Biba is the mirror image for integrity: no read down and no write up, which protects trusted data from contamination. Clark-Wilson is a practical commercial integrity model in which users change data only through approved transactions, with separation of duties and auditing. Brewer-Nash, the Chinese Wall model, changes a user's access dynamically to prevent conflicts of interest. A simple rule helps: if the scenario worries about disclosure, think Bell-LaPadula; about accuracy, think Biba or Clark-Wilson; about competing clients, think Brewer-Nash.

Models are implemented by trusted components. The reference monitor is the concept that every access between a subject and an object is mediated by a component that is always invoked, tamper-resistant and small enough to verify; the security kernel implements it, and the trusted computing base is everything that enforces security. Products can be evaluated under the Common Criteria and given an assurance level from EAL1 to EAL7, which describes how rigorously the product was tested, not how secure it is. Within an organization, certification is the technical evaluation, and accreditation or authorization is management's formal acceptance of the residual risk.

Special environments change the usual assumptions. Industrial control systems prioritize safety and availability and are often unpatchable, so they are segmented from corporate networks. In cloud services, the shared responsibility model means the provider secures the underlying infrastructure while the customer is always responsible for its data, identities and configuration.

Physical security protects everything above it. Mantraps prevent tailgating, lighting and design principles (Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design) shape behavior, and fire protection must match the hazard, with gaseous agents or preaction sprinklers in data centers to limit accidental water damage. Above all, human life safety comes first: exit doors fail open in a power loss, with compensating controls such as alarms and guards, even though that weakens access control.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to security architecture? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Picture designing a bank. You do not rely on one thick door; you build layers: street cameras, a locked lobby, a teller counter, a vault with a time lock, and a safe-deposit box inside (defense in depth). Each employee gets only the keys they need (least privilege), and two people must turn keys together for the vault (separation of duties). If the alarm panel loses power, the doors to the street must still open for anyone fleeing a fire (fail open for life safety), while the vault stays shut. The security models are like different house rules: one says secrets never leave the vault, another says nobody may alter the ledger except through the official forms.",
        'keyTerms': ['Least privilege', 'Defense in depth', 'Fail securely', 'Zero trust', 'Bell-LaPadula', 'Biba', 'Clark-Wilson', 'Brewer-Nash', 'Reference monitor', 'Common Criteria'],
        'commonTraps': "Bell-LaPadula protects confidentiality, Biba protects integrity: do not swap them. A higher assurance level does not mean stronger features. Certification is technical; accreditation is management's risk decision. Zero trust is an approach, not a product to buy. Doors fail open for human safety even though that is less secure. In IaaS the customer, not the provider, secures the guest operating system.",
        'scenario': "A consulting firm is building a system for analysts who serve two competing banks. The architects choose a model that blocks an analyst from viewing the second bank's files after touching the first, require all changes through approved forms, and place the servers in a data center with a mantrap, preaction sprinklers and exit doors that unlock if power fails. A separate team wants to move administrators to a zero trust model.",
        'onTheJob': "When reviewing an architecture, ask what happens when each control fails, and whether any single person or component can defeat the whole design. Insist on threat modeling at design time. Treat vendor assurance claims as evidence to examine, not a guarantee. Walk the physical site yourself: tailgating, propped doors and unattended visitors are found by looking, not by reading policy.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'cryptography-pki',
        'title': 'Cryptography and Public Key Infrastructure',
        'summary': 'Symmetric and asymmetric encryption, hashing, signatures, certificates and the key management that makes any of it trustworthy.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f79', 'f80', 'f81', 'f82', 'f83', 'f85', 'f86', 'f88', 'f87'],
        'quizIds': ['q54', 'q55', 'q56', 'q57', 'q58', 'q59'],
        'reading': """Cryptography transforms information so that only the right people can read it, trust it, or prove who sent it. The CISSP expects you to know the building blocks and, more importantly, which security goal each provides. Symmetric encryption uses one shared secret key, is fast and is used for bulk data; its weakness is distributing that key safely. Asymmetric encryption uses a mathematically linked pair: a public key anyone may have and a private key kept secret. It solves distribution and enables digital signatures, but it is slower. Real protocols combine both: asymmetric methods authenticate and exchange a session key, and a symmetric cipher such as AES protects the data.

AES is the current symmetric standard, with a 128-bit block and keys of 128, 192 or 256 bits; DES and 3DES are obsolete or deprecated. How a block cipher is used matters as much as the cipher: ECB mode encrypts identical blocks identically and reveals patterns, CBC chains blocks using an initialization vector, CTR turns the cipher into a stream, and GCM adds authentication so tampering is detected. Never reuse an IV or nonce with the same key. Among asymmetric algorithms, RSA relies on the difficulty of factoring, Diffie-Hellman lets two parties agree on a shared secret across an open channel (but needs authentication to stop a man in the middle), and elliptic curve cryptography gives equivalent strength with much shorter keys, which suits mobile and embedded devices.

A hash function turns data into a fixed-size digest and is one-way. It provides integrity checking, because any change alters the digest, but not authentication, because anyone can compute it. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collision resistance; SHA-2 and SHA-3 are current. Adding a secret key gives a message authentication code such as HMAC, which proves integrity and that the sender knew the key. A digital signature goes further: the sender encrypts the message hash with a private key, and anyone can verify it with the public key, giving integrity, authentication and nonrepudiation but not confidentiality. For passwords, use a slow, salted hash with a unique salt per password, which defeats rainbow tables.

A public key infrastructure provides trust in public keys. A certificate authority signs X.509 certificates that bind a public key to an identity; a registration authority validates requests; and revocation is published through certificate revocation lists or answered per certificate by OCSP. Trust flows from a root authority that must be kept offline and protected. If a private key is exposed, revoke the certificate and issue a new key pair; waiting for expiry leaves you open to impersonation.

Finally, key management is where cryptography usually fails. Keys must be generated with good randomness, protected in hardware security modules where the stakes are high, rotated, backed up or escrowed under split custody, and destroyed at the end. Dual control and split knowledge ensure that no single person holds a complete key. Looking ahead, quantum computers threaten RSA and elliptic curve algorithms, and NIST has standardized post-quantum algorithms, so organizations with long-lived secrets should inventory their cryptography and plan for agility.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to cryptography? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Symmetric encryption is like a padlocked box where sender and receiver both hold identical keys; fast, but how did you get the second key to your friend safely? Asymmetric encryption is like a public mailbox slot: anyone can drop a letter in (the public key), but only the owner has the key to open it (the private key). A digital signature works the other way round, like a wax seal only you could have made, which anyone can recognize. A certificate authority is the notary who confirms that this particular seal really belongs to this particular person, and the revocation list is the notary's list of seals that were stolen.",
        'keyTerms': ['Symmetric', 'Asymmetric', 'AES', 'Diffie-Hellman', 'Hash', 'HMAC', 'Digital signature', 'Certificate authority', 'OCSP', 'Key management'],
        'commonTraps': "A hash does not authenticate and a MAC does not give nonrepudiation. A digital signature does not provide confidentiality. Key size is not block size. ECB is never the right mode. Diffie-Hellman without authentication is vulnerable to a man in the middle. After a private key compromise, revoke and reissue rather than waiting. Increasing RSA key length is not a defense against quantum computers.",
        'scenario': "A payment company needs to send signed, confidential files to a partner, protect its certificate authority's signing key, and fix a breach in which unsalted password hashes were cracked in hours. The architect also notes that the firm holds records that must stay secret for thirty years.",
        'onTheJob': "Use well-reviewed libraries and standard algorithms; never invent your own. Spend your effort on key lifecycle: where keys live, who can touch them, how they rotate, how you recover from compromise. Maintain a cryptographic inventory, since you cannot migrate to newer algorithms or respond to a weak-algorithm advisory without knowing where each one is used.",
    },
]


LESSONS += [
    {
        'id': 'network-architecture-segmentation',
        'title': 'Secure Network Architecture: OSI, Segmentation and Perimeter Defenses',
        'summary': 'The layered models that describe networks, the devices that operate at each layer, and the designs and tools that contain attackers.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f93', 'f94', 'f95', 'f100', 'f101', 'f102', 'f103', 'f104', 'f109'],
        'quizIds': ['q67', 'q69', 'q70', 'q75', 'q79', 'q80'],
        'reading': """Networks are described in layers so that each layer can be understood and secured separately. The OSI model has seven: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation and Application. The TCP/IP model compresses them into four: link, internet, transport and application. What matters for the exam is knowing where devices and attacks sit. Hubs and cables are Layer 1, switches work with MAC addresses at Layer 2, routers forward packets by IP address at Layer 3, and TCP and UDP port numbers live at Layer 4. ARP spoofing is a Layer 2 attack, IP spoofing is Layer 3, and a SYN flood is a Layer 4 attack. When a question names an attack, work out the layer first and the right control usually follows.

At Layer 4, TCP is connection-oriented and reliable. It opens a connection with the three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) and acknowledges data, while UDP is connectionless and lightweight. An attacker can send floods of SYN packets without finishing the handshake to exhaust the server's connection table, and mitigations include SYN cookies and rate limiting. Other denial-of-service attacks target bandwidth or application resources, and distributed versions come from thousands of sources, so blocking one address is pointless; the defense is capacity and upstream filtering through scrubbing services or a content delivery network.

Good network design assumes that something will eventually be compromised and tries to limit how far an attacker can move. Segmentation divides the network into zones by function or sensitivity, with firewalls or access lists controlling traffic between them. A screened subnet, often called a DMZ, sits between the internet and the internal network and holds public-facing servers so that a compromised web server does not give direct access to internal systems. VLANs create separate broadcast domains on switches, though they are not a security boundary without controls between them; attacks such as VLAN hopping are prevented by disabling unused ports and automatic trunking. Microsegmentation pushes the same idea down to individual workloads and is a core part of zero trust designs.

Firewalls come in several generations. Packet filters examine headers without tracking state. Stateful firewalls remember connections and permit return traffic for sessions the inside initiated. Application proxies and next-generation firewalls inspect traffic at Layer 7, adding application awareness, user identity and intrusion prevention, and a web application firewall protects HTTP applications from attacks such as injection and scripting. Place firewalls at every trust boundary, deny by default, and review rules regularly.

Detection complements prevention. An intrusion detection system monitors and alerts, usually from a copy of the traffic; an intrusion prevention system sits inline and can block. Signature-based detection recognizes known patterns and is accurate, but it misses a zero-day. Anomaly or behavior-based detection compares activity with a baseline and can spot the unknown, at the cost of false positives and tuning. In every design question, the strongest answer is the one that limits blast radius while keeping the business function working.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to network security? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Think of an office building. The OSI layers are like the postal system: the road (physical), the street addressing (data link), the city and zip code (network), the apartment number (transport) and finally the letter itself (application). A screened subnet is the lobby where visitors are met by reception without being allowed into the offices; internal zones are floors that need separate badges; microsegmentation puts a lock on each office. A firewall is the security desk checking who goes where, and an intrusion detection system is the camera room that notices someone behaving oddly, even if they have never been seen before.",
        'keyTerms': ['OSI model', 'TCP', 'UDP', 'Screened subnet', 'VLAN', 'Microsegmentation', 'Stateful firewall', 'WAF', 'IDS', 'IPS'],
        'commonTraps': "Switches are Layer 2 and routers are Layer 3, and a Layer 3 switch routes. A VLAN alone is not a security boundary. Signature-based detection cannot catch a zero-day, and anomaly detection creates more false positives. An IPS is inline and an IDS is not. Distributed denial of service cannot be fixed by blocking one source. Putting public servers on the internal network, or directly on the internet, are both wrong answers when a screened subnet is available.",
        'scenario': "A retailer hosts its storefront on web servers that connect to an internal order database. After an attacker compromised a web server and scanned the internal network, the architect redesigns the network with a screened subnet, separate zones for payment systems and corporate users, and an inline IPS. A holiday flood of traffic from thousands of hosts later takes the site down, even though the firewall rules were correct.",
        'onTheJob': "Draw your network and every trust boundary before you change anything; most surprises come from paths nobody documented. Default-deny between zones, then open only what is justified and owned. Review firewall rule sets quarterly for stale entries. Tune detection tools with your own baselines, and plan for denial-of-service attacks with your provider before the first one arrives.",
    },
    {
        'id': 'secure-communications-wireless',
        'title': 'Secure Communications: Protocols, VPNs, Wireless and Remote Access',
        'summary': 'The encrypted protocols, authentication services and wireless standards that protect data as it moves between people, sites and devices.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f96', 'f97', 'f98', 'f99', 'f105', 'f106', 'f110', 'f114', 'f115'],
        'quizIds': ['q68', 'q71', 'q72', 'q74', 'q76', 'q77'],
        'reading': """Data in transit crosses networks you do not control, so the first rule is to use the encrypted version of every protocol. Telnet becomes SSH, FTP becomes SFTP or FTPS, HTTP becomes HTTPS, LDAP becomes LDAPS, and older SNMP becomes SNMPv3. Transport Layer Security protects web traffic and many other applications; version 1.3 removed weak legacy options, requires forward secrecy so that a later key compromise does not expose past sessions, and shortens the handshake. If a scenario shows credentials crossing the network in clear text, the right answer is almost always the encrypted equivalent of the same service, not a stronger password or a different port.

IPsec protects traffic at the network layer. The Authentication Header provides integrity and authentication but not encryption, while Encapsulating Security Payload adds confidentiality. IKE negotiates the security associations. Transport mode protects the payload between two hosts, and tunnel mode encapsulates the whole original packet between gateways, which is the standard site-to-site VPN. Remote-access VPNs connect individual users, using IPsec or TLS-based designs. Split tunneling sends only some traffic through the tunnel, which saves bandwidth but lets internet traffic bypass corporate inspection and turns a compromised endpoint into a bridge. Zero trust network access improves on broad VPN access by granting per-application access after verifying identity and device posture, and SASE delivers such controls from the cloud.

Authentication for network access is usually centralized. IEEE 802.1X is port-based: a supplicant authenticates through an authenticator, such as a switch or access point, to an authentication server before any network access is granted, and network access control can also check device posture. The server is commonly RADIUS, which uses UDP, combines authentication and authorization and encrypts only the password. TACACS+ uses TCP, separates authentication, authorization and accounting and encrypts the whole payload, so it is favored for administering network devices.

Wireless networks broadcast to anyone nearby, so the standard matters. WEP is broken and must never be used. WPA2 uses AES with CCMP; Personal mode uses one shared passphrase, while Enterprise mode uses 802.1X with individual credentials, which gives accountability and lets you revoke one person at departure. WPA3 replaces the pre-shared key handshake with Simultaneous Authentication of Equals, which resists offline guessing and provides forward secrecy. Hiding the SSID and filtering by MAC address are not real controls. Threats include rogue access points attached to the network and evil twins that imitate a legitimate network; wireless intrusion prevention and mutual authentication address them.

Email and name resolution need their own protections. SPF lists which servers may send mail for a domain, DKIM signs messages, and DMARC tells receivers what to do when checks fail; together they fight domain spoofing but do not encrypt messages, which is the job of S/MIME or PGP. DNSSEC signs DNS records so resolvers can verify authenticity and integrity, while DNS over HTTPS or TLS encrypts queries for privacy but does not prove the data is genuine.""",
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to secure communications? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': "Sending data over a network without encryption is like shouting your credit card number across a crowded room. TLS is stepping into a soundproof booth with the person you are talking to, after checking their ID. A VPN is a sealed armored tunnel between two buildings: outsiders can see that a tunnel exists, but not what travels through it. 802.1X is the guard at the building entrance asking each visitor for personal ID before opening any door, while the shared Wi-Fi passphrase is a single key given to everybody, and you cannot take it back from one person without changing the lock for everyone.",
        'keyTerms': ['TLS 1.3', 'IPsec ESP', 'Tunnel mode', 'Split tunneling', '802.1X', 'RADIUS', 'TACACS+', 'WPA3', 'DMARC', 'DNSSEC'],
        'commonTraps': "AH authenticates but does not encrypt; ESP can do both. DNSSEC gives authenticity and integrity, not confidentiality. SPF, DKIM and DMARC fight spoofing but do not encrypt mail. RADIUS encrypts only the password, TACACS+ the whole payload. Hidden SSIDs and MAC filters are weak. WPA2-Personal's shared key does not give per-user accountability. And split tunneling is a trade of visibility for bandwidth.",
        'scenario': "A professional services firm gives staff full-tunnel VPN access, and administrators log in to switches over Telnet. The office Wi-Fi uses one shared passphrase that a departed employee still knows. Customers have received phishing mail that appears to come from the firm's domain. The architect proposes SSH for management, 802.1X with individual credentials for Wi-Fi, per-application zero trust access for remote staff and SPF, DKIM and DMARC for mail.",
        'onTheJob': "Inventory every clear-text protocol on the network with a capture or flow tool; the list is usually longer than anyone believes. Move wireless to individual credentials early, since changing a shared passphrase is painful after the fact. Roll out DMARC in monitoring mode first, then quarantine, then reject, so you do not block legitimate mail. Keep management interfaces on a separate network and log every administrative session.",
    },
]


